.TH AENIGMA-VPN-CLIENT 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-vpn-client \- configure and start an OpenVPN client connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-vpn-client
.B \-c
.I CLIENT_NAME
.B \-d
.I DOMAIN
.B \-p
.I PORT
.br
.B aenigma-vpn-client
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-vpn-client
installs OpenVPN and
.BR openvpn-systemd-resolved (8)
if necessary, writes an OpenVPN client configuration for a previously
issued client identity, and enables and starts it as a systemd
.B openvpn@
service instance.
.PP
This script is intended to run on the client machine, which is expected
to be
.B separate
from the VPN server. It does not generate or sign any certificates
itself; it expects the client's certificate, private key, the server's
CA certificate, and the shared TLS-auth key to have already been issued
on the server (by a separate client-certificate issuance script) and
copied into place on this machine beforehand.
.PP
.B aenigma-vpn-client
must be run as root, since it creates the unprivileged
.I openvpn
system user if it does not already exist, changes ownership of the
client's private key files, and manages the
.B openvpn@
systemd service.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " CLIENT_NAME"
Identifier of the client identity being configured, e.g.
.IR john .
Must match the name used when the client certificate was issued, and
determines where this script looks for the client's files:
.IR /etc/openvpn/clients/ CLIENT_NAME.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-d " DOMAIN"
Domain name or address of the VPN server to connect to, e.g.
.IR example.com .
Used as the
.B remote
target in the generated configuration and to name the resulting
config file and systemd instance. Required.
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT"
UDP port the VPN server listens on, e.g.
.IR 1194 .
Must match the port the server was provisioned with. Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage information and exit.
.SH PREREQUISITES
Before running
.BR aenigma-vpn-client ,
the following files must already exist under
.IB /etc/openvpn/clients/ CLIENT_NAME
on this machine, copied over from the server (or wherever the client
certificate was issued):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B ca.crt
\(em the server's certificate authority certificate
.IP \(bu 2
.IB CLIENT_NAME .crt
\(em the client's signed certificate
.IP \(bu 2
.IB CLIENT_NAME .key
\(em the client's private key
.IP \(bu 2
.B ta.key
\(em the shared TLS-auth key generated on the server
.RE
.PP
.B aenigma-vpn-client
verifies each of these files is present before making any changes, and
exits with a clear error naming the missing file if one is not found.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the client configuration was written and the
.B openvpn@
service was started.
.TP
.B 1
Invoked without root privileges, called with insufficient or invalid
arguments, the client's file directory does not exist, or one of the
required files listed under
.SM PREREQUISITES
is missing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/clients/ CLIENT_NAME
Directory expected to already contain the client's certificate, private
key, CA certificate, and TLS-auth key (see
.SM PREREQUISITES ).
The private key and TLS-auth key are set to mode 0600, owned by
.IR openvpn ,
by this script.
.TP
.IB /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN .conf
Generated OpenVPN client configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/update-systemd-resolved
DNS update script invoked by the generated configuration's
.B up
/
.B down
hooks, provided by the
.I openvpn-systemd-resolved
package, to keep the system's DNS resolution in sync with what the VPN
server pushes for the duration of the connection.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The generated configuration file is named after
.IR DOMAIN ,
not
.IR CLIENT_NAME .
Running this script again for a different
.I CLIENT_NAME
against the same
.I DOMAIN
overwrites the existing configuration for that domain, switching the
.B openvpn@ DOMAIN
service to use the newly specified client identity instead.
.IP \(bu 2
This script performs no certificate generation, so re-running it for the
same
.I CLIENT_NAME
and
.I DOMAIN
is safe and idempotent: it simply reapplies file ownership/permissions,
rewrites an equivalent configuration file, and restarts the service.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openvpn (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.rujas@aenigma.ro>